body:
## Motivation

A remote planner may not know whether a command was accepted, partially executed, completed, or merely had its receipt delayed. Retrying a pointer move, key sequence, save action or modal confirmation can duplicate side effects. Some current owner probes reject duplicate or stale requests, but there is no general operation-level contract for safe retry and status inquiry across the interface.

Distributed systems address this with idempotency keys, deduplication windows and explicit uncertainty states. For an agent interface, the important question is whether these semantics can reduce planner recovery loops without pretending that arbitrary GUI actions are naturally idempotent.

## Core hypothesis

A bounded idempotency and outcome-query contract can reduce unsafe duplicate actions and unnecessary planner replays after delivery ambiguity.

The flow is:

command with unique intent_id and attempt_id
→ runtime records admission/execution state
→ receipt may be delayed or lost
→ planner queries status instead of blindly retrying
→ retry is admitted only when the operation declares an idempotency policy
→ unknown side effects remain UNKNOWN and require observation/oracle recovery

Candidate outcomes include ACCEPTED, RUNNING, COMPLETED, REJECTED_DUPLICATE, NOT_FOUND, FAILED_BEFORE_INPUT and EFFECT_UNKNOWN.

## Why existing Issues do not already cover it

- #17 separates safety control from telemetry, but does not define retry semantics when telemetry is missing.
- #15 records causal lineage, but does not make command identity and deduplication an execution contract.
- #16 handles transactional grouping and compensation, not ordinary retry of one operation.
- #20 repairs malformed schemas, not delivery ambiguity after a valid command.
- #23 schedules independent intents, while this issue handles repeated attempts for the same intent.

If the design becomes only more provenance fields, merge it into #15. If it assumes every GUI action can be safely replayed, reject it.

## Candidate design

An operation declares:

- intent_id: semantic work item identity;
- attempt_id: one submission attempt;
- operation_id: runtime execution identity;
- idempotency class: SAFE_RETRY, DEDUPLICATE, REQUIRES_EFFECT_CHECK, NON_RETRYABLE;
- deduplication scope and retention;
- status-query capability;
- effect uncertainty policy.

The runtime must distinguish:

- command received;
- accepted;
- input started;
- input completed;
- effect observed;
- independent task result.

A repeated attempt with the same intent_id must not automatically replay a DEDUPLICATE operation. A new attempt may be allowed only after a status result or a fresh effect check. NON_RETRYABLE operations return uncertainty rather than a false “not executed” result.

## Safety and correctness constraints

- IDs do not grant authority or bypass freshness, focus, surface or lease checks.
- Deduplication state is bounded and its expiry is explicit.
- Expired deduplication must not silently turn an old command into a new valid command.
- A status query has no task side effect and does not refresh input authority.
- Unknown outcome cannot be converted to FAILED_BEFORE_INPUT without evidence.
- Cleanup/release commands retain independent safety semantics.
- The runtime must preserve evidence of partial execution and failed receipt delivery.
- Idempotency classes are operation-specific and must default conservatively.
- Universal GUI fallback remains available; no application API is required for command identity.

## Smallest falsifiable experiment

Use the existing desktop/Calc task and a deterministic terminal task. For each, inject:

- delayed receipt after accepted input;
- lost ordinary receipt;
- timeout after input starts;
- duplicate submission before and after completion;
- retry after lease expiry;
- same intent_id with altered parameters;
- reused attempt_id across sessions.

Compare:

A. current retry behavior;
B. unique IDs with duplicate rejection;
C. IDs plus status query and operation-specific retry policy.

Independently inspect saved text/workbook values and forbidden collateral effects. Include a non-retryable operation where the correct result is EFFECT_UNKNOWN.

## Metrics

Primary:

- duplicate side-effect rate;
- unsafe replay rate;
- false claims of no execution or completion;
- final task correctness;
- planner boundaries needed to resolve delivery ambiguity.

Secondary:

- status-query success;
- recovery time;
- deduplication memory/retention cost;
- stale-ID rejection;
- operation-specific false rejection;
- model-visible token/byte overhead;
- behavior under cross-session and reordered receipts.

## Failure modes and red-team questions

- A GUI operation can be non-idempotent even when its low-level input is identical.
- Deduplication may suppress a legitimate new action after state has changed.
- A lost status record can force the same uncertainty as before.
- IDs may create false confidence without application-effect verification.
- Retention windows may become a memory/privacy burden.
- The proposal may be only #15 provenance or #17 telemetry separation.
- Status queries may add more planner boundaries than they save.
- Two different operations may share an intent accidentally.
- A save or modal operation may have external effects that cannot be rolled back.

The experiment must include altered-parameter retries and cross-session ID reuse as hard negative controls.

## Promotion and rejection rule

Promote only if fresh delayed/lost-receipt cases show fewer duplicate side effects and fewer recovery boundaries than the current baseline, while preserving task correctness and correctly retaining UNKNOWN outcomes. Require zero execution of altered-parameter duplicates under the same intent identity.

Reject or defer if the benefit depends only on a reliable receipt channel, if status cannot distinguish before-input from unknown effect, if deduplication suppresses valid work, or if it is indistinguishable from #15.

## Relationship to current evidence

The current repository distinguishes accepted, terminal, release and delivery/flush records, and recent journal-stall work demonstrates that safety can succeed while notification delivery is blocked. That creates exactly the uncertainty in which a remote agent is tempted to retry. This issue asks whether the interface can make retry a typed, auditable operation rather than an implicit planner behavior.
